---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import { readCacheControl } from "../lib/profile-read.ts";

// What the server keeps, written from the code (DESIGN.md → Components & States → Data page). Each
// statement describes code as it is today. Change any of it, re-read this page:
//   web, from packages/web: the schema (migrations/), sign-in and revoke (src/lib/auth.ts,
//     src/lib/github.ts, src/lib/users.ts, src/pages/api/v1/auth/*), publish and delete
//     (src/pages/api/v1/profile.ts), the public read, its cache header and the rename redirects
//     (src/pages/api/v1/u/[handle].ts, src/lib/profile-read.ts, src/pages/[handle]/index.astro,
//     src/pages/[handle]/vs/[viewer].astro), what a page shows (src/components/Profile.astro,
//     src/components/Diff.astro), the log lines (every console.error, and wrangler.jsonc's
//     observability), the rate limits (src/lib/rate-limit.ts, and infra/waf-ratelimit.sh at the
//     repo root), browser storage (src/components/Layout.astro, src/components/TerminalRun.astro),
//     fonts and no sessions (astro.config.mjs);
//   cli, from packages/cli/src: the local files (token-store.ts, update-check.ts,
//     dismissals.ts), sign-in, revoke and logout (device-flow.ts, auth-http.ts, index.ts),
//     detection (detect.ts), what a publish sends and when it asks (api.ts, commands.ts), the CI
//     and same-server checks (config.ts), ymmv update (update.ts).
// What the code can't show (Cloudflare's own log contents and retention, how far back its D1
// backups reach, and which Workers plan the account is on) is named as outside it, never promised.
// The backup window is from developers.cloudflare.com/d1/reference/time-travel and the plan is the
// account's, both as of 2026-10-02. A plan change re-reads the backup paragraph.
//
// Server-rendered on purpose: a prerendered page is a static asset, which Workers Static Assets
// serves ahead of the Worker, so the middleware's www/http redirect and HSTS would never see it.
// `privacy` is in RESERVED_ROUTES (@ymmv/shared), so no handle can claim this path.
// Request-invariant HTML that names hashed /_astro assets: the short policy, like the landing.
Astro.response.headers.set("cache-control", readCacheControl("notfound"));

const REPO = "https://github.com/ymmv-fyi/ymmv";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "What the ymmv.fyi server stores when you sign in and publish, what it doesn't store, and what ymmv delete removes.";
---

<Layout
  title="ymmv.fyi/privacy"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
>
  <article class="doc">
    <div class="url"><a href="/">ymmv.fyi</a>/<h1 class="handle">privacy</h1></div>

    <p class="doc-lede">
      This page lists what ymmv.fyi and its CLI keep about you. It is written from the code, not
      as a legal policy, and the code is public at <a href={REPO}>github.com/ymmv-fyi/ymmv</a> if
      you want to check a line.
    </p>

    <h2>What the server stores</h2>
    <p>The database (Cloudflare D1) keeps your data in four tables:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>users</code>: your GitHub account id, your GitHub username as your handle, your extra
        lines, when you last published (<code>updated_at</code>) and when you first signed in
        (<code>created_at</code>). Signing in creates this row, even if you never publish.
      </li>
      <li><code>profile_entries</code>: the stack values you published, one row per key.</li>
      <li>
        <code>tokens</code>: one row per sign-in, with a SHA-256 hash of the token, your account id,
        and when the token was made and revoked. The server stores the hash, never the token.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>handle_history</code>: a handle you stopped using by renaming on GitHub, with your
        account id and the date. After you rename on GitHub and sign in again, the old address
        redirects to the new one while your profile is published.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Tokens don't expire. Each one works until its row is revoked or deleted:{" "}
      <code>ymmv logout</code> revokes the one on that machine, signing in again revokes the one it
      replaces, and <code>ymmv delete</code> deletes every token row on your account, live or
      revoked. A revoked row stays in the table until <code>ymmv delete</code> deletes it.
    </p>

    <h2>What the server doesn't store</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Your GitHub access token.</b> During sign-in the CLI gets one from GitHub and sends it to
        the server once. The server asks GitHub which account it belongs to, keeps the id and
        username from the answer, and drops the token. The CLI doesn't save it either.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Your email address, name or avatar.</b> The server keeps only your account id and
        username from GitHub's answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Your IP address, browser or referrer.</b> None of them goes into the database or into
        the server's own log lines.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Sign-in asks GitHub for no OAuth scopes, which limits that GitHub token to read-only access
      to public information. Neither the CLI nor the server revokes it afterwards. To remove ymmv's
      access on GitHub's side, revoke it at{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/settings/applications">github.com/settings/applications</a>. That
      doesn't sign you out of ymmv. <code>ymmv logout</code> signs out the machine you run it on.
    </p>

    <h2>Logs and rate limits</h2>
    <p>
      The server writes a log line only when a request fails. A few of those lines name the handle
      or GitHub account id involved, and none contains a token. They go to Cloudflare Workers Logs,
      which <code>wrangler.jsonc</code> turns on. Workers Logs also keeps its own record of each
      request, and this code doesn't set what Cloudflare puts in that record or how long it keeps
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      To rate-limit requests, Cloudflare counts sign-ins and the CLI's signed-in calls per IP
      address, and sign-ins, publishes and deletes per GitHub account id. The counters live in
      Cloudflare, not in the database.
    </p>

    <h2>What is public</h2>
    <p>
      Your page at <code>ymmv.fyi/&lt;handle&gt;</code> is public, and so is the same data as JSON
      at <code>ymmv.fyi/api/v1/u/&lt;handle&gt;</code>, which any website can fetch. Both show your
      handle, your stack, your extra lines and when you last published. Neither shows your GitHub
      account id. A diff page, <code>ymmv.fyi/&lt;handle&gt;/vs/&lt;you&gt;</code>, shows your
      values next to someone else's.
    </p>

    <h2>What deleting your profile removes</h2>
    <p>
      <code>ymmv delete</code> asks first, unless you pass <code>-y</code>. Then, in one step, it
      deletes every row that carries your GitHub account id, in all four tables:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your <code>users</code> row,</li>
      <li>your <code>profile_entries</code> rows,</li>
      <li>your <code>tokens</code> rows, live and revoked, so every machine is signed out,</li>
      <li>
        your <code>handle_history</code> rows, so an address you renamed away from stops
        redirecting.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      After that, no table holds a row with your GitHub account id. Your page and its JSON answer
      404 until you sign in and publish again. Signing in again makes a new <code>users</code> row,
      the same as a first sign-in.
    </p>
    <p>
      The delete doesn't reach Cloudflare's backups. Cloudflare keeps point-in-time backups of the
      database, which it calls Time Travel and describes at{" "}
      <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/reference/time-travel/">developers.cloudflare.com/d1/reference/time-travel</a>.
      A restore puts the whole database back to a chosen moment, rows deleted since then included.
      In October 2026 Cloudflare's docs put the limit at 7 days back on the Workers Free plan and 30
      days on Workers Paid, and ymmv.fyi's account was on the Free plan. Nothing in this code runs
      a restore. A maintainer would have to run one by hand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing purges a cache on delete. Pages, JSON and rename redirects go out with{" "}
      <code>s-maxage=30, stale-while-revalidate=86400</code>, so a cache that follows those
      headers can serve the old copy for up to a day. Copies someone already saved are out of the
      site's reach.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>ymmv logout</code> does less. It revokes the token on that machine and deletes the local
      copy, and your page stays up.
    </p>

    <h2>In your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The site sets no cookies and loads no analytics or third-party scripts. Its fonts are
      downloaded when the site is built and served from ymmv.fyi.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site keeps two keys in <code>localStorage</code>: <code>ymmv-theme</code>, your light or
      dark choice, and <code>ymmv-you</code>, the last handle you submitted in a diff form. When the
      home page plays its intro, it sets <code>ymmv-landing-run</code> in{" "}
      <code>sessionStorage</code> so the intro plays once per visit.
    </p>

    <h2>On your machine</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI keeps three files in <code>~/.config/ymmv</code> on Linux (or{" "}
      <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ymmv</code>), <code>~/Library/Preferences/ymmv</code> on macOS and{" "}
      <code>%APPDATA%\ymmv\Config</code> on Windows:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>token.json</code>: your ymmv token, handle, GitHub account id and the server address.
        On Linux and macOS only your user can read it (mode 0600).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>update-check.json</code>: when the CLI last checked for a new version, and what it
        found.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>dismissed-marks.json</code>: detection mismatches you told it to stop pointing out.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Detection runs on your machine and only fills in the preview. A publish sends what the
      preview showed: your handle, your stack values and your extra lines. In a terminal{" "}
      <code>ymmv</code> asks before it sends, and <code>ymmv -y</code> sends without asking.{" "}
      <code>ymmv set</code> and <code>ymmv unset</code> publish their change right away, with no
      preview and no confirmation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Besides ymmv.fyi, the CLI talks to github.com when you sign in. It asks registry.npmjs.org for
      the latest version once a day, or six hours after a failed check. Set{" "}
      <code>YMMV_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> or <code>NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=1</code> to turn that off, and
      it never runs under CI. <code>ymmv update</code> runs your package manager, which talks to its
      own registry.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Ask in a GitHub issue at <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>github.com/ymmv-fyi/ymmv/issues</a>. For
      anything sensitive, such as a security problem or something you'd rather not post in public,
      use the private advisory form at{" "}
      <a href={`${REPO}/security/advisories/new`}>github.com/ymmv-fyi/ymmv/security/advisories/new</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
